From 5e150c90311bd403e6eacb721edd1352071c802a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AmirSalahY <amir@noury.ch>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 15:39:37 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 26/34] feat(alias): pick up alias changes without restarting
 the session

The alias table was read once at startup, so a user who edited their
rc file and sourced it, or just typed an alias at the prompt, kept
completing against the old set until they opened a new session. The
prompt-start hook already knows what command was executed, so it is
also the point at which the table can be known stale: reload after
anything that defines or removes a name, or sources a file that might.
Unawaited like the startup read, since it re-evaluates a whole profile
and must not sit between the command finishing and the next prompt.

Reloading only ever added names, so an unalias left the old expansion
in place; the table is now rebuilt instead of merged.

reinit reports what the shells actually expose, and which bash startup
files were readable. An alias living in a file no shell reads produces
no error anywhere, and looks identical to one that failed to parse.
